# No Silent Fails

A silent `return` / `return false` that swallows a programming error makes bugs invisible.

## Rule

Every early return that represents a **contract violation** (something that should never happen in correct code) must be preceded by an `assert`.

A silent return is only acceptable for **expected runtime states** (e.g. feature disabled, no data yet).

## Pattern

```cpp
// ❌ BAD — caller gets no signal that something is wrong
if (!m_device || !m_renderEncoder)
    return false;

// ✅ GOOD — fires immediately in debug, still safe in release
assert(m_device      && "MyFunc: device is nil");
assert(m_renderEncoder && "MyFunc: no active render encoder");
if (!m_device || !m_renderEncoder)
    return false;
```

## Hot-path exception

For functions called **every frame**, prefer a single combined assert so the message is still clear:

```cpp
assert(m_hdrEnabled && "DoBloomPass: HDR enabled but bloom resources are nil");
if (!m_bloomBrightRT || !m_bloomBlurHRT)
    return;
```

## Distinguish contract violations from soft skips

| Situation | Pattern |
|-----------|---------|
| Nil pointer that should never be nil (`gRenDev`, device, PSO after init) | `assert` + `return` |
| Feature disabled at runtime (HDR off, fog off) | silent `return` is fine |
| Init failure already logged upstream | silent `return` is fine (error was reported at the source) |
| PSO lookup returning nil (registry not populated) | `assert` — this is always a bug |

## Downcasts

Use `checked_cast<T>` (defined in `MetalBaseRenderer.m`) instead of bare `static_cast` for all `gRenDev` downcasts. It runs `dynamic_cast` in debug and `static_cast` in release.

```cpp
// ❌ BAD
CMetalRenderer* r = static_cast<CMetalRenderer*>(gRenDev);

// ✅ GOOD
CMetalRenderer* r = checked_cast<CMetalRenderer>(gRenDev);
assert(r && "MyFunc: gRenDev is not a CMetalRenderer");
```
